    "seo": {
      "title": "History & Modern Iran: Regions, Empires and Political Change | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iran's history through archaeology, regional dynasties, cultural exchange, constitutionalism, oil politics, and the revolution of 1979."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "reading-iranian-history",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A country, a cultural world, and a changing map",
        "content": "The word Iran can describe a modern state, a geographical setting, or a cultural and linguistic world. Those meanings overlap, but they are not interchangeable. A history confined to present borders would exclude important centers of Persian-language culture; a history of Persian culture alone would overlook the other languages and communities of the country. Encyclopaedia Iranica explicitly distinguishes these geographical, political, linguistic, and cultural uses. [s1]\n\nThis distinction changes how the past should be read. The existence of cultural connections does not establish continuous political unity, and an ancient kingdom should not be mapped automatically onto a modern nation. The most useful starting point is therefore a set of questions: where did a particular authority operate, whose language appears in its records, and what kinds of evidence survive? Iran's history becomes more intelligible when these questions remain separate rather than being answered by a single story of uninterrupted national continuity. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "archaeology-and-empires",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Before and beyond the imperial capitals",
        "content": "Early urban societies offer a different entrance to Iranian history from the familiar sequence of kings. Susa, in the southwest, preserves evidence of settlement, administration, and exchanges between Mesopotamia and the Iranian plateau. Its excavated buildings include residential, religious, and palatial structures. These remains show why a city can connect several historical periods without belonging to one unchanging civilization. [s3]\n\nThe ancient Persian empires form another major branch of the story. Achaemenid, Parthian, and Sasanian history concerns large political formations whose territories and relationships extended beyond today's Iran. Their histories require more than accounts of conquest: inscriptions, administrative practices, religion, and contact with neighboring powers also matter. The seventh-century Arab conquest of Sasanian Iran provides a major chronological dividing point, although a political transition should not be treated as the instantaneous replacement of every language, institution, or social practice. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "medieval-cultural-geography",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Regional power and the Persian-language world",
        "content": "Medieval Iran cannot be understood as a simple succession of governments occupying the same territory. The Tahirids governed Khorasan, an extensive eastern region, while the Saffarids emerged in Sistan. Their relationships with the Abbasid caliphate—the wider Islamic political authority centered in Baghdad—differed considerably. Regional autonomy and recognition by the caliph could coexist; conflict with that authority was another possibility. [s4]\n\nThe Samanids illustrate why cultural geography matters. Their principal centers included Bukhara and Samarqand, now in Uzbekistan. Their courts supported Persian poetry, historical writing, and translation alongside Arabic learning. The history of Persian literature consequently crosses modern frontiers. Court patronage helped sustain this activity, but the political map remained divided and contested. Cultural production should neither be mistaken for political unity nor reduced to a list of rulers' achievements. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "safavid-transformation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Religion and state formation",
        "content": "The Safavid accession in 1501 marked an important transformation. Shah Ismail established Twelver Shiism as the state religion, distinguishing his realm from major Sunni neighbors. This was a political and religious project involving coercion, warfare, and institution building, rather than evidence that the population had always shared a uniform confession. [s5]\n\nSafavid government also depended on negotiating among different sources of power. The Qizilbash, tribal military supporters of the dynasty, and Persian administrators supplied different resources and competed for influence. Royal authority rested partly on managing those relationships. This makes the period useful for understanding a recurring historical problem: announcing sovereign power and exercising it effectively were different things. The later-dynasties branch follows such changes in authority and political organization. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "constitutionalism-and-oil",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Who should govern, and who should control resources?",
        "content": "The Constitutional Revolution of 1905–1911 established a parliament and constitutional monarchy. Its participants debated the relationship between representative institutions, royal power, religion, and law. The movement was internally diverse: constitutionalism did not mean exactly the same thing to every cleric, merchant, intellectual, or political organizer who supported it. Its achievements were followed by reversals and renewed struggles. [s6]\n\nOil brought sovereignty and foreign intervention into particularly sharp focus. Nationalization in 1951 challenged British control of the industry. A surviving United States memorandum from November 1952 records British proposals for a coup against Prime Minister Mohammad Mosaddegh and American discussion of its risks. That document establishes deliberation at that moment; it does not, by itself, describe the entire subsequent operation. [s7]\n\nMosaddegh was overthrown in August 1953 with British and American involvement and the participation of Iranian opponents. Historians debate the relative weight of foreign operations and domestic political forces. Recognizing that debate does not erase the documented intervention. The outcome strengthened Mohammad Reza Shah's authority and became a lasting reference point in arguments about independence and political legitimacy. [s8]",
        "source_ids": ["s6", "s7", "s8"]
      },
      {
        "id": "revolution-and-historical-method",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Revolution, institutions, and unfinished coverage",
        "content": "The revolution of 1978–1979 ended Pahlavi monarchy. Manchester University Library's collection of contemporary newspapers exposes the diversity of political organizations and expectations surrounding it. The library's introduction emphasizes both profound socioeconomic change under the shah and continuing scholarly disagreement over the revolution's causes. Contemporary newspapers are valuable records of what participants said and imagined, but their claims still require comparison and contextual reading. [s9]\n\nConstitutional documents provide a complementary record. Syracuse University's Iran Data Portal identifies the constitution adopted in 1979 and its amendment in 1989, alongside earlier constitutional texts and assembly debates. These materials document institutional design and argument; they should not be assumed to demonstrate how every institution operated in practice. [s10]\n\nThe subject branches below extend this approach beyond political chronology to economic history, social life, science, and historical figures. This introductory draft does not yet provide an adequately researched account of the Iran–Iraq War or developments after 1989. Those omissions prevent it from serving as a complete account of modern Iran through the research cutoff.",
        "source_ids": ["s9", "s10"]
      }
    ],
