    "description": "Ancient societies in the territory of modern Iran developed through distinct regional histories and exchanges with neighboring lands. Cities, cemeteries, workshops, and administrative remains provide different kinds of evidence, and the site's excavation context often matters as much as the objects themselves.",
    "tags": ["Ancient Iran", "Archaeology", "Early cities", "Bronze Age", "Iron Age", "Regional societies"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-26T11:49:13Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-26T11:49:13Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Ancient Civilizations of Iran: Cities, Regions and Evidence | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand early Iran through Elam, Shahr-e Sukhteh, Marlik, Jiroft, and Lorestan, with attention to regional differences and archaeological uncertainty."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "different-kinds-of-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An archaeological landscape, not a single civilization",
        "content": "The subjects grouped here are not equivalent units. Elam names a changing historical and political formation; Shahr-e Sukhteh is an excavated urban settlement; Marlik is principally known as an elite cemetery. Treating all three as identically organized 'civilizations' would obscure what their evidence actually establishes. The distinctions are especially important when comparing settlement organization, political authority, and social hierarchy. [s1][s2][s3]\n\nA city can reveal the arrangement of houses and workshops, while a cemetery shows choices about burial and the objects deposited with the dead. Neither supplies a complete picture of society on its own. Marlik's scholarly assessment accordingly calls for further study of nearby settlement remains to understand political organization and domestic life. Its rich graves cannot simply stand in for the everyday experience of an entire population. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "southwest-and-east",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Urban life in different regions",
        "content": "Susa offers a long archaeological sequence at a meeting point between the southwestern lowlands, Mesopotamia, and the Iranian plateau. Excavated administrative, residential, and monumental structures make it possible to examine urban change across successive political settings. Its importance does not make it a template for every other early settlement in Iran. [s4]\n\nIn eastern Iran, Shahr-e Sukhteh—'Burnt City,' called Shahr-i Sokhta in UNESCO's listing—provides a different urban record. UNESCO dates its foundation to approximately 3200 BCE and describes occupation in four principal periods until approximately 1800 BCE. Separate areas for housing, manufacture, burial, and monuments support an account of differentiated urban space. Its documented connections reached the Indus region, Central Asia, and southern maritime zones. [s2]\n\nThese connections make isolation an unhelpful model. Early communities participated in exchanges of materials, techniques, and cultural practices. Nevertheless, a shared object type or a trading connection does not establish common government. UNESCO's account of Shahr-e Sukhteh supports interregional contact; it does not establish that those connected regions formed one state. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "northern-and-western-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Cemeteries, metalwork, and the limits of labels",
        "content": "Marlik lies in the western Caspian basin, in a different setting from the southwestern urban centers and eastern desert-margin settlements. Its elite burial ground belongs to the late second and early first millennia BCE. Its identity as a cemetery is central to interpreting its celebrated vessels and other grave goods: these are evidence of funerary selection and social display, rather than a neutral inventory of all material life. [s3]\n\nThe archaeological sites of Lorestan raise another distinction. 'Luristan bronzes,' the conventional English art-historical term, describes a particular body of decorated metalwork. It does not encompass every bronze excavated in the region. The label has also been applied incorrectly to objects from other places or periods. Controlled finds associate much of the canonical material with tombs, while certain decorated pins occur as offerings in a sanctuary. These contexts support different interpretations of use. [s5]\n\nThe regional branch therefore concerns both places and the history of how their objects have been classified. An attractive museum object can preserve evidence of craft skill while lacking the information needed to reconstruct its original setting. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "jiroft-and-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why excavation context matters",
        "content": "The label 'Jiroft' requires particular care. Archaeologist Oscar White Muscarella's survey criticized the tendency to assign objects from several plundered cemeteries to a single site or unified culture bearing the modern city's name. His intervention is a warning about evidential limits, not a complete assessment of every later excavation or claim. [s6]\n\nExcavation context means the recorded position and associations of a find: the building, grave, layer, and neighboring objects with which it occurred. Without those associations, even an authentic artifact may be difficult to date or interpret securely. Looting therefore removes information as well as objects. The Jiroft and Lorestan literature demonstrates why claims about kingdoms, chronology, and cultural boundaries must distinguish controlled discoveries from antiquities-market material. [s5][s6]\n\nThis guide preserves the established page names while explaining what each label can support. Elam, urban Shahr-e Sukhteh, funerary Marlik, the Jiroft region, Tepe Sialk, and Lorestan should be approached through their particular records. The Tepe Sialk component still requires fuller source inspection before this overview is ready for publication.",
        "source_ids": ["s5", "s6"]
      }
    ],
