  {
    "schema_version": "1.0",
    "page_id": "topic-1-1-1",
    "page_path": "/explore/history/ancient-civilizations/elam/",
    "name": "Elam",
    "level": 3,
    "parent_path": "/explore/history/ancient-civilizations/",
    "entity_type": "concept",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Elam was a changing constellation of kingdoms and cultural traditions associated especially with southwestern Iran and its neighboring highlands. Its history connects Susa and Anshan with Mesopotamia, while its language and traditions continued into the Achaemenid period.",
    "tags": ["Elam", "Susa", "Anshan", "Ancient Iran", "Elamite language", "Tchogha Zanbil"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-26T11:49:13Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-26T11:49:13Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Elam: Susa, Anshan and a Changing Ancient World | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Discover Elam's changing geography, religious architecture, language, and relationships with Mesopotamia and Achaemenid Persia."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "a-changing-geography",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Where was Elam?",
        "content": "Elam should not be imagined as a country with fixed borders throughout antiquity. François Vallat's historical survey emphasizes that its extent varied with both period and observer. Mesopotamian descriptions could use the name broadly, while political relationships among lowland and highland centers changed. Equating Elam permanently with Susiana, the southwestern plain around Susa, therefore produces a misleading map. [s1]\n\nAnshan is essential to correcting that picture. Its identification with Tal-e Malyan in Fars places a major Elamite center in the highlands, beyond the Susiana plain. Susa and Anshan were connected, but their relationship cannot be reduced to a timeless capital ruling an unchanged territory. The history concerns shifting combinations of centers, dynasties, and neighboring powers. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "history-and-sources",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A history reconstructed from uneven records",
        "content": "Elamite history survives unevenly. Vallat notes that reconstruction depends heavily on Mesopotamian documentation because indigenous historical sources are limited. That imbalance matters: a neighboring ruler's account of conquest records a political claim from a particular side, rather than an impartial description of Elamite society. [s1]\n\nThe familiar division into Old, Middle, and Neo-Elamite periods helps organize a long record, but does not imply uninterrupted centralized government. Susa alternated between different political relationships, including subordination to Mesopotamian powers. In the Middle Elamite period, rulers using the title 'kings of Anshan and Susa' linked the highlands and lowlands in their presentation of authority. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "religious-architecture",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Tchogha Zanbil and monumental patronage",
        "content": "Tchogha Zanbil, also known as Dur-Untash, makes Middle Elamite royal ambition tangible. Founded by King Untash-Napirisha in the thirteenth century BCE, it was a religious center whose principal monument was a ziggurat: a massive stepped structure associated with temple worship. UNESCO identifies its dedication to the divinities Inshushinak and Napirisha. [s2]\n\nThe surrounding complex included temples, royal residences, and enclosure walls. These remains show the scale of royal religious patronage and the organization of monumental space. They do not, by themselves, reveal how all households lived or how widely a royal religious program was accepted. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "language-and-continuity",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Language outlasted independent rule",
        "content": "Elamite is not an early form of Persian. Its relationship to other language families has not been established with certainty. The cuneiform corpus—texts written using wedge-shaped signs—extends from the third millennium into the Achaemenid period. Scholars distinguish Old, Middle, Neo-Elamite, and Late or Achaemenid Elamite, with substantial changes within the language over that span. [s3]\n\nWriting system and language must also be distinguished. The label 'Proto-Elamite' identifies an early script and should not be treated as proof that every text using it has a securely identified language. Older scholarship's assessments of undeciphered scripts require particular care as research develops. [s3]\n\nThe rise of Persian imperial rule therefore did not erase every Elamite cultural practice. Elamite continued in written use under the Achaemenids. Elam's significance lies partly in this combination of political change and cultural persistence: it belongs to the history of Iran without needing to be recast as an early Persian nation-state. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "evidence-map",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Three complementary routes into Elam",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Evidence for Elam\"] -->|\"Historical reconstruction uses\"| B[\"Mesopotamian accounts\"]\n  A -->|\"Architecture survives at\"| C[\"Tchogha Zanbil\"]\n  A -->|\"Language is documented in\"| D[\"Elamite cuneiform texts\"]\n  B -->|\"Requires attention to\"| E[\"The neighboring ruler's viewpoint\"]\n  C -->|\"Documents\"| F[\"Royal religious building\"]\n  D -->|\"Shows continued use into\"| G[\"The Achaemenid period\"]\n```",
        "summary": "These arrows identify kinds of evidence and what they can establish. They describe neither ancient administrative reporting nor a continuous political hierarchy.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
